.\" shrink(1): the reference for bin/shrink. install.sh links it to
.\" ~/.local/share/man/man1/shrink.1, which man finds through ~/.local/bin on
.\" PATH. test/lint.sh fails when a flag shrink's help lists is missing here.
.TH SHRINK 1 2026-11-21 dotfiles "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shrink \- make a smaller copy of videos, images and PDFs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shrink
.RI [ options ]
.IR file ...
.br
.B shrink \-h
.br
.B shrink \-\-help
.RI [ type ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shrink
makes a
.I Shrunk copy
of each
.I file
next to it and leaves the original untouched:
.I a.mov
becomes
.IR a.shrunk.mp4 .
It picks the type from the extension, in any case, and prints one line per
file: its size, the copy's size and how much smaller it is.
.PP
A flag beats its
.B SHRINK_*
variable, which beats the default, so a Mac can set its own defaults in
.IR ~/.zshrc.local ,
such as
.BR "export SHRINK_CRF=28" .
.SH OPTIONS
A flag that takes a value also takes it after
.BR = ,
as in
.BR \-\-crf=28 .
.B \-\-
ends the options, so a file whose name starts with
.B \-
can follow it.
.TP
.BI \-o " path"
Write into the folder
.IR path ,
or, for one file, to the file
.IR path ,
which must end in the format the input comes out in.
.TP
.B \-f
Replace an existing output, and shrink
.I *.shrunk.*
files too, which are skipped otherwise.
.TP
.B \-n
Print the commands instead of running them.
For a video it still runs
.BR ffprobe ,
which only reads, since the commands depend on the video's size and frame rate.
.TP
.B \-h
A short usage screen.
.TP
.BR \-\-help " [\fItype\fR]"
Every setting for
.I type
.RB ( video ,
.B image
or
.BR pdf ),
or for every type, with the commands run underneath.
.SS Video
.TP
.BI \-\-crf " n"
Quality, 0\(en51; lower is better and bigger.
.BR SHRINK_CRF ,
default 23.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-res " n"
Caps the short side at
.IR n ,
an even number; never enlarges.
.BR SHRINK_MAX_RES ,
default 1080.
.TP
.BI \-\-fps " n"
Caps the frame rate; never raises it.
.BR SHRINK_FPS ,
default 30.
.TP
.BI \-\-audio\-bitrate " rate"
AAC stereo bitrate, such as 96k.
.BR SHRINK_AUDIO_BITRATE ,
default 128k.
.SS Image
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-edge " n"
Caps the long edge; never enlarges.
.BR SHRINK_MAX_EDGE ,
default 2560.
.TP
.BI \-\-quality " n"
1\(en100: JPEG and WebP quality, and the top of pngquant's quality range for
a PNG.
.BR SHRINK_QUALITY ,
default 80.
.SS PDF
.TP
.BI \-\-pdf " level"
.BR screen ,
.B ebook
or
.BR printer :
images at about 72, 150 or 300 dpi (Ghostscript's presets).
.BR SHRINK_PDF ,
default ebook.
.SH TYPES
.SS Video
.IR mov ,
.IR mp4 ,
.IR m4v ,
.IR mkv ,
.IR avi ,
.I webm
come out as an H.264 and AAC
.IR .mp4 ,
8\-bit 4:2:0, with the metadata kept and +faststart:
.PP
.RS
ffmpeg \-i IN \-map_metadata 0 \-c:v libx264 \-crf N \-pix_fmt yuv420p
[\-vf caps] \-c:a aac \-b:a R \-ac 2 \-movflags +faststart+use_metadata_tags OUT
.RE
.PP
While stderr is a terminal it shows ffmpeg's progress line.
.SS Image
.IR jpg ,
.IR jpeg ,
.IR png ,
.IR heic ,
.IR heif ,
.IR webp ,
.IR tif ,
.I tiff
keep their format and extension as written
.RI ( photo.JPG
becomes
.IR photo.shrunk.JPG ),
except HEIC, HEIF and TIFF, which become
.I .jpg
so they open anywhere.
ImageMagick resizes them and sets the quality:
.PP
.RS
magick IN \-resize NxN\e> \-quality Q OUT
.RE
.PP
A PNG goes through
.B pngquant
(a lossy palette) instead of
.BR magick 's
.BR \-quality ,
and its EXIF chunk, which
pngquant drops on a Mac, is copied back.
Metadata stays, the EXIF orientation tag included, and pixels aren't rotated,
with two exceptions: a HEIC comes out upright with orientation 1, and a TIFF is
turned upright and loses its EXIF.
.SS PDF
.I pdf
comes out as a
.I .pdf
through Ghostscript, with metadata as Ghostscript writes it:
.PP
.RS
gs \-q \-dSAFER \-dPDFSTOPONERROR \-sDEVICE=pdfwrite \-dPDFSETTINGS=/LEVEL
\-o OUT IN
.RE
.PP
A damaged PDF fails rather than coming out incomplete.
.SH SAFETY
.IP \(bu 2
The original is never changed.
.IP \(bu 2
The copy is written to a hidden
.I .part
file next to it and renamed when done, so an interrupted or failed run leaves
nothing half written.
.IP \(bu 2
A result that isn't smaller is deleted and reported, and the run carries on.
A PDF that's mostly text often comes out no smaller.
.IP \(bu 2
An existing output is refused without
.BR \-f ,
and two inputs in one run never write the same output.
.IP \(bu 2
.I *.shrunk.*
inputs are skipped without
.BR \-f .
.IP \(bu 2
The copy keeps the original's modification time.
.IP \(bu 2
A missing tool fails only the files that need it, naming the tool and
.BR install.sh .
.SH HDR
iPhones record 10\-bit HDR video.
When ffprobe reports a PQ (HDR10, Dolby Vision) or HLG transfer function,
.B shrink
warns on stderr and shrinks anyway:
.PP
.RS
shrink: a.mov is HDR (HLG); the shrunk copy is standard range and will look
washed out
.RE
.PP
The warning shows with
.B \-n
too and doesn't change the exit status.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every file was shrunk, skipped as a Shrunk copy, or left alone because the
result wasn't smaller.
.TP
.B 1
At least one file failed: an unsupported type, a missing file or tool, a
refused output, or a tool's error.
The other files are still shrunk.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an unknown option, a bad setting, a bad
.B \-o
path or
.B \-\-help
type, or no files.
.TP
.BR 129 ", " 130 ", " 143
Interrupted by SIGHUP, SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B shrink a.mov b.mp4
Writes
.I a.shrunk.mp4
and
.IR b.shrunk.mp4 .
.TP
.B shrink \-o ~/Desktop *.mov
Puts every copy in
.IR ~/Desktop .
.TP
.B shrink IMG_0042.HEIC
Writes
.IR IMG_0042.shrunk.jpg .
.TP
.B shrink \-\-pdf screen report.pdf
Writes a small
.I report.shrunk.pdf
for the screen.
.TP
.B SHRINK_CRF=28 shrink \-n a.mov
Prints the ffmpeg command at CRF 28 instead of running it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR magick (1),
.BR pngquant (1),
.BR gs (1)
